Many people came to him with paralyzed, blind, lame, deaf, and many other sick people, and he healed them all.
And people were amazed when they saw the lame walking, the lepers being cleansed, and the blind receiving their sight; and they glorified the God of Israel. Jesus fed four thousand men (Mark 8:1-10)
Jesus called his disciples to him and said, "I feel sorry for these people; they have already been with me three days and have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry, or they may collapse on the way."
The disciples said to him, "Where can we get enough bread in this deserted place to feed such a crowd?"
Jesus asked them, "How many loaves do you have?" They said, "Seven loaves and a few small fish."
And Jesus ordered his disciples to sit down on the ground.
He took the seven loaves and gave thanks and broke them and gave them to his disciples, and they distributed them to the people.
They all ate until they were full, and left seven large baskets full of food behind.
There were four thousand men among them, besides women and children.
After sending off the people, he got in his boat and sailed to Magdel.
The signs of the times (Mark 8:11-13; Luke 12:54-56)
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus, asking him for a sign from heaven.
Jesus answered them, “When it’s evening you say, ‘It’s going to be fair weather, for the sky is red.’
In the morning you say to yourselves: "It's going to rain today because it is red in the west." You know enough about the weather to understand these things, but not about men.
A wicked generation seeks signs, but even if a sign were given to them, they would still say, "It's just like Jonah's miracle." And he left them and went away. The yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees (Mark 8:14-21)
The students reached the opposite shore but forgot to bring bread with them.
Jesus said to them, "Watch out! Be careful of the yeast that comes from the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The students discussed among themselves and said: "This is because we don't have bread."
But Jesus knew what they were saying and said to them, "Why do you discuss among yourselves about having no bread? Do you still not understand or comprehend?"
Bro, can't you understand? Bro, don't you remember the five loaves that fed 5000 people and how many baskets full of leftovers were collected?
And how many baskets did you fill with the seven loaves that fed four thousand people?
How can you fail to understand that I am talking about bread? Watch out for the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees!”
At that time they understood what he meant when he said "Watch out for the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees." Peter declared, "You are the Christ" (Mark 8:27-30; Luke 9:18-21)
When Jesus came to the region of Caesarea Philippi, he asked his disciples, "Who do people say that I am?"
They replied, "Some say John the Baptist; others say Elijah; and still others, Jeremiah or one of the prophets."
He asked them, "Who do you say I am?"
Simon Peter answered, "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus said to him, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven."
And I tell you this: You are Peter, which means rock, and upon this rock I will build my church, and even if hell attacks it, nothing will be able to destroy it.
I will give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven, whatever you bind on earth shall be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven."
And at that time he ordered his disciples to tell no one about him being Christ. Jesus first told them about his death and resurrection (Mark 8:31; 9:1; Luke 9:22-27)
And at that time Jesus began to show his disciples that he must go to Jerusalem and suffer many things from the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and on the third day be raised.
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "Never, Lord!" he said. "This shall never happen to you!"
But Jesus turned to Peter and said, "Get behind me, Satan! You're an obstacle to me. You don't have in mind the concerns of God, but merely human concerns."
At that time Jesus said to his disciples, "If anyone wants to come after me, let him deny himself and take up his cross and follow me."
Whoever wants to save his life will lose it; whoever loses his life for my sake will find it.
What good is it for someone to gain the whole world yet forfeit their soul? Or what can anyone give in exchange for their soul?
I am the son of man who will come with my angels in my father's glory, and at that time I will reward every person for what they have done.
I tell you the truth, some who are standing here will not taste death before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.”
Jesus appears in his glory to his disciples (Mark 9:2-13; Luke 9:28-36)
After six days Jesus took with him Peter and James and John his brother, and led them up a high mountain by themselves.
He changed in front of them, his face became like the sun and his hair turned white like light.
And suddenly they saw Moses and Elijah talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will make three shelters—one for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
And while he was still speaking, a bright cloud overshadowed them, and a voice came from the cloud saying, "This is my Son, whom I love; listen to him!"
When they heard this thing, the disciples fell down on their faces and were terrified.
But Jesus came up to them and touched them, saying, "Get up; don't be afraid."
They just shrugged their shoulders and said they didn't see anyone but Jesus alone.
And when they came down from the mountain, Jesus ordered them, "Don't tell anyone what you have seen until after the Son of Man has risen from the dead."
The disciples asked him, "Then why does the book of the Jews say that Elijah must come first?"
Answer them: "Indeed Elijah will come and he'll set everything right."
"But I will take you to Elijah at Horeb, where he was when God spoke with him. And they did whatever they wanted to him. And I, son of man, am also going to be tortured by them."
And at that time, the disciples understood what he was telling them about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy possessed by an unclean spirit (Mark 9:14-29; Luke 9:37-43)
And when Jesus was come near, he saw a man sitting at the receipt of custom, and saith unto him, Follow me.
Oh God have mercy on my son, he suffers from epilepsy and is tortured so much, falling in fire and water all the time.
And I brought him to your disciples, but they couldn't heal him."
Jesus replied, "What kind of generation is this? Why do you keep asking for signs? I tell you the truth, no sign will be given to it."
Jesus cast out the demon, it came out of him, and the boy was cured at once.
The disciples came to Jesus privately and asked him, "Why couldn't we drive it out?"
He said to them, “Because of your little faith. I tell you the truth, if you have faith as small as a mustard seed, you can say to this mountain, ‘Move from here to there,’ and it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you.”
# 17:21 There are ancient texts that contain this verse: "But this kind does not go out except by prayer and fasting." Jesus again tells about his death and resurrection (Mark 9:30-32; Luke 9:43-45)
When the disciples were gathered together in Galilee, Jesus said to them: "The Son of Man is about to be handed over to men."
And they will kill me, but I'll rise again on the third day." Come on guys, don't worry so much. Jesus paid the temple tax
They went to Capernaum, where some tax collectors came up to Peter and said, "Doesn't your teacher pay temple taxes?"
He answered, "Yes." And he said to me, "Go home, before Jesus speaks up. He'll say, 'What do you think, Simon? From whom do earthly kings collect taxes or tribute—from their own children or from foreigners?'"
Peter said: "From Bethsaida." Jesus replied: "Then you're free men, sons of your country."
But we don't want to cause them trouble. Go to the lake and cast your line, catch whatever fish you can get, bring it back here, and I'll open its mouth - there you'll find enough silver coins to pay for both our taxes. Take that money and settle my tax dues and yours."
